  "body": " Calendar  Below is a calendar with dates that you should study each topic and dates to take the written exams. Depending on your schedule, you may wish to do some of the work on weekends. Just be sure to not fall behind and be prepared for assessments on the prescribed dates.   Course Schedule    Week  Sun\/Mon   Tue\/Wed   Thu\/Fri  Saturday    1  1.1 What can we expect;  1.2 Finding solutions to linear systems   1.3 Computation with Sage  1.4 Pivots and their influence on solution spaces  Review & Exam 1    2  2.1 Vectors and linear combinations; 2.2 Matrix multiplication and linear combinations   2.3 The span of a set of vectors; 2.4 Linear independence   2.5 Matrix transformations; 2.6 The geometry of matrix transformations  Review & Exam 2    3  3.1 Invertibility  3.2 Bases and coordinate systems  3.3 Determinants; 3.4 Subspaces  Review & Exam 3    4  4.1 An introduction to eigenvalues and eigenvectors  4.2 Finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors  4.3 Diagonalization, similarity, and powers of a matrix  Review & Exam 4    5  5.1 Definition of vector spaces  5.2 Linear combinations, span, and independence; 5.3 Vector subspaces  5.4 Basis and dimension  Review & Exam 5    6  6.1 The dot product  6.2 Orthogonal complements and the matrix transpose  6.3 Orthogonal bases and projections  Review & Exam 6    7  6.4 Finding orthogonal bases  6.5 Orthogonal least squares  7.1 Symmetric matrices and variance  Review & Final Exam     Exams will take place during a time period from Friday at 6 PM until Sunday at 11:59 PM at the end of each week of the course. They will cover the following material from the course.   Exam dates and topics     Exam     Topics     Exam 1   Sections 1.1-1.4    Exam 2   Sections 2.1-2.6    Exam 3   Sections 3.1-3.4    Exam 4   Sections 4.1-4.3    Exam 5   Sections 5.1-5.4    Exam 6   Sections 6.1-6.3    Final Exam   All sections     This course is accelerated! That means it moves at more than double the pace of a normal semester. The reality is that to be successful, several hours a day will need to be dedicated to your studies. Studying on weekends to get ahead, especially if holding a job or other commitments during the work week, will be helpful. Below is a table with time you might expect to spend on each aspect of the course (for each section of the textbook). Note that this totals roughly 4 hours per day or 20 hours per week! In reality, this is likely the bare minimum you should expect to spend working on this course each week.   Estimated Time on Various Aspects of the Course     Item    Estimated Time Per Section     Reading Textbook  30-60 minutes    Watching Videos  15-30 minutes    Working the Activities in Text  60 minutes    Written Homework  60-90 minutes    Electronic Homework (MOMwork)  30 minutes     "
